ow process of learning how to live.</p><p>My stories rarely revolve around saving the world. They&#39;re interested in the people who live in it — the ones who are imperfect, lost, and trying to find their place anyway.</p><p>Some of my projects start with careful planning. Others start with a single idea that refuses to leave. Either way, I usually discover the story alongside my readers rather than arriving with it fully formed — what you&#39;re reading is often close to a first draft, mistakes and all.</p><p>I write as a hobby, in whatever free time I can find around everything else. That means release schedules shift, and I take real breaks after finishing a big chunk of work — it&#39;s practically tradition at this point. But I don&#39;t start things to abandon them. If a story&#39;s still going, expect it to keep going, even if slowly.</p><div class="section-divider" style="margin:48px 0;"><span class="asterism"><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span></span></div><h3>Why these kinds of stories</h3><p>Enigmatic Pathways Mystic Circuits started after an anime watch session turned into a real conversation about what family should mean beyond raw talent. Re:Zero and Mushoku Tensei both left a mark on how I think about second chances and slow, hard-won emotional growth — not power fantasies, but people learning to carry what happened to them.</p><p>Summoned by Mistake, I Decided to Learn How to Live became the place for ideas that didn&#39;t fit anywhere else — something looser and more relaxing to write. The Shadow I Cast Over Two Beautiful Flowers exists for a different reason entirely: I wanted proof, mostly to myself, that I could take a story to a real, satisfying ending.</p></div>',1)),e("aside",k,[t[1]||(t[1]=e("div",{class:"avatar-large",style:{"background-image":"url('/assets/images/profile.png')"}},null,-1)),t[2]||(t[2]=e("h4",null,"The name",-1)),t[3]||(t[3]=e("p",{style:{"font-size":"0.9rem",color:"var(--ink-soft)","m